---
import GuideLayout from '@layouts/GuideLayout.astro';
import ReferenceBlock from '@components/ReferenceBlock.astro';
import { config } from './config';
import { toolPath, categoryPath } from '@lib/paths';
import { toolPathBySlug } from '@lib/tools/tool-link';
import { categories } from '@data/categories';
import type { EcosystemEntry } from '@data/types';

interface Props {
  title: string;
  description: string;
  readMinutes: number;
  updatedAt: string;
  entry: EcosystemEntry;
}

const props = Astro.props;
const category = categories.find(c => c.slug === config.categorySlug)!;
const toolHref = toolPath({ slug: config.slug, segment: category.segment });
const categoryHref = categoryPath(category);
---

<GuideLayout
  {...props}
  toolSlug={config.slug}
  toolName={config.name}
  toolCategorySlug={config.categorySlug}
>
  <section id="quick-answer">
    <h2>What is connect 4 same device? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      Connect 4 is a 7 by 6 board for two people.
      Four of one color in a line wins.
    </p>
    <p>
      You play connect 4 online on this page by passing one phone.
      That is how you play connect 4 same device, and connect 4 no download means the page is the whole game.
    </p>
    <p>
      Connect four two player is the same match.
      A four in a row game counts a row, a column, and a diagonal of four.
    </p>
    <a href={toolHref} class="cta-link">Open Connect 4</a>
  </section>

  <section id="why-one-device">
    <h2>Why does one phone matter? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      One phone matters because both players are already in the room.
      A hot seat keeps the match there.
    </p>
    <p>
      Passing one phone across a table when nobody has paper is the ordinary case.
      Playing a full match without creating an account is the other reason to stay on this page.
    </p>
    <p>
      Who drops first is a separate question.
      A <a href={toolPathBySlug('coin-flipper')}>coin flip</a> settles it, and
      <a href={toolPathBySlug('tic-tac-toe')}>tic tac toe</a> is the smaller board when four is the wrong target.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="how-a-drop-works">
    <h2>How does the disc drop? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      The disc drops into the lowest empty cell of the column under your finger.
      The column lights up first, and the disc commits when you release.
    </p>
    <p>
      For example, X releases on the left column and the disc sits on the bottom row.
      O releases on the next column.
      X returns to the left column, and the new disc sits on top of the first.
    </p>
    <p>
      For example, four releases by X in that same column, with O answering beside it, make a vertical line.
      The banner says X wins.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="rules">
    <h2>What are the four in a row rules? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      The rules are one color, four cells, one straight line.
      A diagonal counts the same as a row or a column.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>X drops first, then O answers</li>
      <li>A disc falls to the lowest empty cell</li>
      <li>Four matching discs in a line end the game</li>
      <li>A full board with no line is a draw</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      For example, discs on a diagonal from the bottom of one column up to the right are a win when they share a color.
      Checking a diagonal before calling the board a draw is part of reading the result.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="hot-seat">
    <h2>How is a hot seat different from a network match? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      A hot seat is two people sharing one phone.
      A network match looks for someone on another device.
    </p>
    <p>
      You pass the phone instead of waiting in a lobby.
      Unlike a network match, the other color is not a computer.
    </p>
    <ReferenceBlock type="key-idea" heading="The other color is a person">
      <p>The other color is a person, so the page never drops a disc for them.</p>
    </ReferenceBlock>
    <p>
      Waiting for a computer to play the other color is the wrong picture of this page.
      This board lives in the <a href={categoryHref}>games category</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="wrong-column">
    <h2>What if the disc lands in the wrong column? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      Undo takes that disc back, and only that disc.
      Taking back a tap that landed in the wrong column returns the turn as well.
    </p>
    <ReferenceBlock type="common-mistake" heading="Aiming at the next column">
      <p>
        Dropping the disc in the column next to the one you aimed at happens when you release after the finger has slid.
        Watch the lit column, then release.
      </p>
    </ReferenceBlock>
    <ReferenceBlock type="common-mistake" heading="Missing a diagonal">
      <p>
        Reading only the rows and missing a diagonal of four leaves a finished game looking open.
        Check both diagonal directions before you call a draw.
      </p>
    </ReferenceBlock>
    <p>
      Avoid refreshing to start over and assuming the session score will vanish.
      The tally stays on this device, and the grid starts empty.
    </p>
    <p>
      Rematch clears the board and hands the first drop to X.
      However, wins and draws from this session stay on the phone.
    </p>
  </section>
</GuideLayout>

<style>
  .cta-link {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    height: var(--touch-target);
    padding-inline: var(--space-5);
    margin-top: var(--space-5);
    background: var(--color-accent);
    color: var(--color-text-inverse);
    font-weight: var(--font-weight-semibold);
    font-size: var(--text-base);
    border-radius: var(--radius-md);
    text-decoration: none;
    transition: var(--transition-color);
  }
  .cta-link:hover { opacity: 0.9; text-decoration: none; }
</style>
